<!-- General -->

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#336699><IMG SRC="images/336699.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><B>General</B></TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Does using the timer violate the EULA?</SPAN><BR>
The timer is a standard user interface modification, which is completely allowed and supported by SOE. It is absolutely legal to use. For more information on modifying the user interface, go to <A TARGET=_blank HREF="http://eqlive.station.sony.com/manual/manual.jsp?id=49368">http://eqlive.station.sony.com/manual/manual.jsp?id=49368</A>.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Will the timer work with other UI mods?</SPAN><BR>
Yes, in general, the timer will work with other mods simply by dropping it into the same folder. The exception being: if the timer uses a window that is also modified by the other mod, AND the replaced file defines elements that are referenced by other files in the mod, you will need to move those elements to one of the other files in the mod.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Can I include the timer as part a mod that I'm distributing to others?</SPAN><BR>
You may include the timer code in your mod, as long as the appropriate credit is given in the description/readme for your mod. Please also include the URL to this site so that users may customize the timer for their own needs, since desired configurations vary from user to user.

<!-- Setup -->

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#336699><IMG SRC="images/336699.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><B>Setup</B></TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>I'm having trouble downloading the files, what should I do?</SPAN><BR>
Internet Explorer 5.5/6.0 is strongly recommended, so try downloading the timer using that if you encounter any problems. If you have a download manager such as Download Accelerator, try holding down the ALT key as you click the download button, to temporarily disable the download manager.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How do I install the timer?</SPAN><BR>
The instructions are included in the ZIP file that contains the graphics file. This file can also be accessed at <A HREF="instructions.html">http://www.horizonsoutpost.com/eq/instructions.html</A>.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How do I move the timer window?</SPAN><BR>
First, make sure that the window isn't locked. To toggle the locked state of the window, right-click anywhere on the window and use the <B>Lock</B> option in the context menu. To move the timer, left-click and hold on a part of the window where the move cursor appears (edges work well for this), then drag the window. Once the window is moved to the desired location, you can lock it to prevent accidental moving.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How do I change the hotkey for the window?</SPAN><BR>
Open the options window, then click on the <B>Keyboard</B> tab. There, you can configure the hotkey for the desired window (the help window has no hotkey). For windows that are opened by typing a command, create a hotkey with the appropriate macro: bug window - <B>/bug</B>, feedback window - <B>/f</B>, gems window - <B>/gem</B>, notes window - <B>/not</B>.</TD></TR>

<!--
<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>When I reload the UI, it changes my ability hotkeys to numbers!</SPAN><BR>
Currently, whenever the UI is reloaded, all ability hotkey labels change to numbers. This is a problem with the UI that only Verant can fix, so sending /bug or /feedback may help. In the meantime, you can restore your ability hotkeys by zoning or logging back in.</TD></TR>
-->

<!-- Slider Timer -->

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#336699><IMG SRC="images/336699.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><B>Slider Timer</B></TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How do I start timing an event with the slider timer?</SPAN><BR>
Simply move the slider to point to the current position of the bar. The bar continues to move, and starts over when it reaches the end. Therefore the bar will reach the slider's position again when exactly one full duration of the timer has elapsed.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Can you give me some examples on how the timer could be used?</SPAN><BR>
Let's say that you want to time an ability like mend, which is available every 6 minutes. So you would create a timer with a 6-minute duration. Whenever you use mend, move the slider to the current position of the bar. It will take the bar exactly 6 minutes to reach the slider again. Or if you want to time a buff like Spirit of Wolf, which lasts 36 minutes, you would make a 36-minute timer. Whenever you cast Spirit of Wolf, move the slider to the current position of the bar. As the bar approaches the slider again, you'll know when the buff is about to wear off.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How can I use the slider timer to time spawns? Each area has a different spawn time.</SPAN><BR>
For timing variable-duration events like spawns, make a general-use timer with a nice round duration (like 30 minutes, 1 hour, or 2 hours). For example, with the duration set at 1 hour, each major tick mark will represent 10 minutes, so you can use that to determine how much time has elapsed.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>For short-duration events, I don't have time to move the slider.</SPAN><BR>
Moving the slider is strictly optional. If you are timing an event with a very short duration, just glance at the bar and make a mental note of its position.</TD></TR>

<!--
<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>I still can't get the move cursor to show up!</SPAN><BR>
Due to the transparent overlapping elements in version 3.1 (and earlier) of the slider timer, there are only a few areas where you can click and drag the window. The best place to click is the bottom edge of the window (left and right edges will also work, but top edge will not). Starting with version 3.2, all edges will work for dragging.</TD></TR>
-->

<!-- Dial Timer -->

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#336699><IMG SRC="images/336699.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><B>Dial Timer</B></TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How is the tick dial different from a regular clock face?</SPAN><BR>
A regular clock face divides one minute into twelve 5-second intervals, while the tick dial divides one minute into ten 6-second intervals. It was designed this way because things in EverQuest are based on ticks (each tick is 6 seconds long).</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How does the tick marker work with the dial?</SPAN><BR>
The tick dial allows you to visually eyeball the duration of an event. When an event begins, you simply glance at the dial and make a mental note of what tick it's on, so you'll know when the event will end. If you don't want to keep track in your head, you can use the tick marker to mark the event's beginning or end by clicking on one of the dial's number labels.</TD></TR>

<!-- Digital Timer -->

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#336699><IMG SRC="images/336699.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><B>Digital Timer</B></TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How do I reset the digital timer?</SPAN><BR>
The digital timer can be reset by typing <B>/load <I>FOLDERNAME</I> 1</B> (short for /loadskin). This basically reloads the UI, which causes all animations to restart from the first frame. The "1" parameter is required if you want to keep your current window settings.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>How do I make a macro to reset the digital timer?</SPAN><BR>
In the Actions window, click on the Socials tab. Right-click on a slot. Give the macro a name, such as "Reset", and type <B>/load <I>FOLDERNAME</I> 1</B> into the first line. After accepting the changes, the macro can be activated by left-clicking, or you can left-click and hold to drag it to a hotkey slot.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Is there any way to reload just the timer window?</SPAN><BR>
Unfortunately, there is currently no way to reload just one window, so reloading the entire UI is the only way of resetting the digital timer. If this is a problem for your system, you might consider using the slider timers (see above), which never need to be reset.</TD></TR>

<!-- Enhancement Requests -->

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#336699><IMG SRC="images/336699.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><B>Enhancement Requests</B></TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Why can't there be start/stop/reset buttons?</SPAN><BR>
Due to the limitations placed on player-made UI mods, is not possible to control the timer. UI mods can only modify the appearance of the UI, not add new functionality. Therefore, the timer is not a "program", it is simply a collection of animated images.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>Can the timer be made to show system time, or Norrath time?</SPAN><BR>
Since the timer is simply a collection of animated images, it does not have any connection to RL time or EQ time. It is only capable of cycling through its frames continuously.</TD></TR>

<TR><TD HEIGHT=1 BGCOLOR=#CCCCCC><IMG SRC="images/cccccc.gif" WIDTH=1 HEIGHT=1></TD></TR>
<TR><TD><SPAN CLASS=subsectiontitle>What are the limitations of animated images in the UI?</SPAN><BR>
Once the EverQuest UI loads up, all animations begin cycling through their frames - there is no way to pause an animation or force it to start from a particular frame at a particular time. Reloading the entire UI via /loadskin will reset an animation, but it also resets all the animations at the same time, so there is no way to reset each animation individually. For more detailed information, please read the following:<BR>
<DIV STYLE="padding: 24px">
<HR>
<P><FONT FACE="verdana, arial, helvetica" SIZE=2><B>Impossible enhancements</B><BR><BR>Here is a list of requests I 
frequently receive. If you have a question along the lines of the ones listed 
here, please read this entire article carefully, as it will explain why your 
request is not possible.<BR><BR>Can you add a reset button that restarts the 
timer?<BR>Can you add pause/resume buttons for the timer?<BR>Can you add a 
button that moves the slider automatically to the position of the bar?<BR>Can 
you start the timer automatically when I cast my spell or use my ability?<BR>Can 
the timer resume from where it left off between sessions?<BR>Can the timer show 
system time, or Norrath time?<BR>Can you add new commands (like /timer start) 
that let me control the timer with macro hotkeys?<BR>Can you add 
functions/variables I can access with my own UI mod?<BR>Can the timer be set up 
to automatically execute in-game commands at specified 
intervals?<BR><BR>Unfortunately, the answer to all of these questions is no. 
These enhancements are impossible for animation-based timers due to the limitations of UI 
mods.<BR><BR><B>Player-made UI mods</B><BR><BR>First, I'll start with some 
general information on UI mods for those who are unfamiliar with the 
capabilities and limitations of UI mods.<BR><BR>Player-made UI mods can only 
alter the appearance of the UI, they cannot add any new functionality. So, you 
can change the size, color, position, etc. of various elements in the UI, and 
you can add things like text labels and images (including animations - more on 
this later) to be displayed, as well as standard UI controls. However, you 
cannot add any actual functionality, such as new commands, or new buttons that 
execute commands. Anything that has functionality in the UI is hard-coded in the 
EverQuest client (i.e., only Sony can add new functionality).<BR><BR>Another 
important related point is that UI mods are not "programs". There are no 
function calls, no variables, no way to execute any commands or detect when 
particular events occur. UI mods consist of only two simple parts: XML data 
files, which contain information loaded by the EverQuest UI engine, and graphics 
files, which contain images used by the mod. The XML data files must contain 
only those nodes allowed by Sony's pre-defined schema, and they specify the 
various attributes of the UI's appearance only (see previous 
paragraph).<BR><BR><B>Animations</B><BR><BR>An animation is basically an image 
composed of multiple frames, which are displayed in sequence. Each frame 
consists of a fixed duration and an image that is displayed for the extent of 
that duration. An animation always starts with the first frame, and continues 
through its list of frames. After the last frame has been displayed, a 
repeating-type animation will start over from the first frame.<BR><BR>As soon as 
the EverQuest UI loads up (when you log in or whenever you type /loadskin), all 
animations in the UI begin displaying each of their frames in sequence. There is 
no way to pause an animation or force it to start from a particular frame at a 
particular time. Reloading the UI via /loadskin will reset all animations, 
causing them to start over from the first frame. This, however, freezes the 
display temporarily and takes too long on some systems, and does not allow 
animations to reset independently of each other.<BR><BR><B>UI 
Timer</B><BR><BR>Based on the above information, one can see that my timer mod 
is not a program at all - it is nothing more than a collection of animations. 
Depending on which type of timer you choose, each animation is either a bar, 
dial, or numerical digit. These animations cannot be controlled and have no 
connection to in-game events.<BR><BR>Due to the limitations and lack of ability 
to control animations, the slider and tick marker were introduced for the bar 
and dial timers, respectively (these markers must be manually set, as they have 
no awareness of or connection to the animations). These give the user the 
ability to manually "mark" the animation, allowing you to begin timing at any 
point during the animation's cycle. With these, the timers can be used without 
mentally keeping track of when timing started or resorting to reloading the 
UI.<BR><BR>I hope this article has been helpful for those who have been wondering 
about possible enhancements or why the timer is designed this way. If you have 
any additional questions please feel free to let me know.</FONT></P>
